---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../i18n/routes';
const lang = 'en';
---
<PageLayout title="Cookies on Stamp Duty Australia: what this site stores and why" description="Cookies on this stamp duty site: none from the calculators, Microsoft Clarity for audience measurement with a notice and opt-out, what the share link holds and how to clear it." lang={lang} noindex={true} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Cookies' }]} showAuthor={false}>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Cookies</h1>
    <p>The calculators themselves set no cookie: they run in your browser and keep nothing once the tab is closed. The site sets no advertising cookie.</p>
    <h2>Audience measurement: Microsoft Clarity</h2>
    <p>To see which state pages and concessions people actually use, the site loads Microsoft Clarity, from Microsoft Ireland Operations Limited. A notice at the bottom of the page says so on your first visit, as Australian Privacy Principle 5 expects, and lets you opt out. Clarity records anonymous sessions and heatmaps: pages opened, clicks and scrolling. The price, state and buyer details you enter are masked in the browser before anything is sent. Its cookies are <code>_clck</code> (one year) and <code>_clsk</code> (one day); Microsoft may set <code>MUID</code> and <code>CLID</code> on its own domains.</p>
    <p>Your answer to the notice is kept in your browser’s <code>localStorage</code> as <code>consent-choice</code>. Choose “Opt out”, now or later through “Cookie settings” in the footer, and Clarity is no longer loaded; clearing the site data in your browser removes cookies already set.</p>
    <p>When you change a calculator, the address bar is updated with your inputs so that you can copy a link to the same result. That link is not sent anywhere by the site; it is shared only if you share it.</p>
    <p>The <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy policy</a> explains who receives Clarity data and for how long.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
